VERDICT: PASS -- VRF-Lite proven on hardware: two VRFs on one device isolate two
         Layer 3 interfaces completely, the isolation is attributable to VRF
         membership and to nothing else, overlapping addresses live in both VRFs at
         once, static inter-VRF routing opens exactly what it names, and the whole
         configuration survives a real reboot. Every cross-VRF result is a real
         far-side frame count on a second host NIC, not a ping and not a `show`.
      isolation between VRFs .......... 5/5 -> 0/5 (identical frames; the far-side
                                                   capture is EMPTY, not merely low)
      the DUT's own L3 interface in
        the other VRF ................. 5/5 -> 0/5 (unreachable from the other VRF)
      attributable to VRF membership .. proven    (BOTH SVIs back in ONE VRF -> 5/5
                                                   again, nothing else changed)
      an interface in exactly one VRF . confirmed (`show ip vrf`; a second VRF is refused)
      the global table shrinks ........ confirmed (the two /24s leave it on the move)
      address unset on a VRF move ..... confirmed (the device says so, and the
                                                   interface stanza is empty after)
      ARP entries are VRF aware ....... confirmed (`show arp vrf red` / `vrf blue`)
      an LO interface per VRF ......... automatic (lo1, lo2; neither was asked for)
      overlapping addresses ........... 5/5 both  (one address answering on two ports
                                                   in two VRFs; each opposite
                                                   capture EMPTY)
      static inter-VRF routing ........ 0/5 -> 5/5 (and the unrouted control stays 0:
                                                   the opening is route-specific)
      removal ......................... clean     (config, route tables, interface
                                                   rendering and dataplane all return
                                                   frame-for-frame to the baseline)
      persistence across a reload ..... holds     (VRFs, loopbacks, separated route
                                                   tables, isolation AND inter-VRF
                                                   routing all survive, from cold ARP)
CAVEAT:  One measured limit of the proof, recorded in STEP 6: removing a static
         inter-VRF route removes it from running-config and from the RIB while the
         DUT KEEPS FORWARDING across the VRF boundary to a destination whose ARP
         entry was resolved while the route existed. It was still forwarding ~2
         minutes and three legs after the route was removed; HOW LONG THE STALE
         ENTRY WOULD SURVIVE UNAIDED WAS NOT MEASURED. This concerns the REMOVAL of
         a deliberate inter-VRF route, not VRF-Lite's isolation, which was complete
         and immediate in every cold-start leg (STEPS 3 and 8a).
         Scope: this is single-device VRF-Lite -- two VRFs, one Layer 3 interface in
         each, on one DUT. Inter-VRF communication was exercised only by the guide's
         static inter-VRF route mechanism; its other named mechanism, dynamic
         filtered route leakage via BGP, was not exercised here, and no route
         distinguisher was configured (Default RD reads `--` throughout). VRF-Lite
         is licensed: the TPS places it under AT-FL-x960-01 and not under x960 Base,
         so this result holds for a unit carrying that licence -- on a Base-only
         x960 the feature would be absent. ICMP destination-unreachable generation
         is rate-limited on this platform, so where type 3 packets are quoted the
         DESTINATION named inside them is the attribution and their count is not a
         measurement.

