TOPOLOGY AS TESTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  This case is the DUT and the two host NICs. Nothing else participates.

   tb277 eth3  00:0d:b9:55:77:0e
     crafted source 192.168.10.5 / 00:11:22:33:44:01
     ARP responder for 192.168.10.5
        |
        v   ICMP echo requests, vlan10, next hop e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 (the DUT's SVI MAC)
   port1.0.10   vlan10 192.168.10.1/24   -- in VRF red while the case is isolated
  +==============================================================+
  |   swi_d   AT-x960-56YSQ   *** DUT ***                        |
  |   the only device this case configures                       |
  |   ip vrf red  (lo1, vlan10)     ip vrf blue  (lo2, vlan48)   |
  +==============================================================+
   port1.0.48   vlan48 192.168.48.1/24  -- in VRF blue while the case is isolated
        |
        v   routed and L2-rewritten by the DUT, COUNTED AS THEY ARRIVE
   tb277 eth4  00:0d:b9:55:77:0f
     ARP responder for 192.168.48.2 / 00:11:22:33:44:02 (and 192.168.48.3 in STEP 6c)

   STEP 7b reverses this: the frames are crafted on eth4 and eth3 is the capture.

  THE INSTRUMENT, and why it is stronger than a reply. Both host NICs land on the
  DUT, on the two segments this case isolates from each other. So the cross-VRF
  reading is a REAL FAR-SIDE COUNT: frames are crafted on eth3 and counted as they
  ARRIVE on eth4, already routed and L2-rewritten by the DUT (source MAC = the DUT's
  SVI MAC, destination MAC = the resolved next hop). A reply would only show that
  something answered; a far-side count shows that the DUT forwarded.

  DUT      : swi_d (AT-x960-56YSQ) console u2 @115200, S/N A2WQS700B,
             sin_x96056-20261012-24. All SVIs on this platform share ONE MAC,
             e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -- which matters in STEP 7.
  host     : tb277 eth3 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e -> swi_d port1.0.10 (vlan10)
             -- the sending segment, and the capture for reply-counted flows
  host     : tb277 eth4 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f -> swi_d port1.0.48 (vlan48)
             -- the FAR SIDE: where every cross-VRF frame is counted on arrival
  global   : vlan41 10.0.41.1/30 and vlan61 10.0.61.1/30 remain in the global VRF
             domain throughout and appear in the route-table output below, which is
             part of the STEP 2 evidence. Neither carries any traffic in this case.
  STP      : port1.0.10 and port1.0.48 Designated Forwarding, re-read before every
             counted leg and again after the reboot of STEP 8 before measuring.
  tools    : tools/arpleg.py (frame craft, `--watch` far-side capture, ARP responder,
             count), tools/ckcon.py, tools/cfg.py, tools/ckyn.py, tools/qmark.py,
             tools/ckreload.py, tools/rc2cfg.py -- all run on tb277 under
             python3.8.

  THE FLOWS. Every counted leg sends the same set, 5 frames each, in one
  invocation, so every reading carries its own controls:
    192.168.48.2   SUBJECT. A fictitious host on the vlan48 segment, COUNTED ON eth4.
                   This is the cross-VRF flow the case turns on.
    192.168.48.1   SUBJECT-2. The DUT's OWN vlan48 address -- "a Layer 3 interface in
                   another VRF domain on the same device", which is exactly what
                   claim G2 says must become unreachable. Counted as echo replies.
    192.168.48.3   SUBJECT-3, in STEPS 6c and 6d only. A second host on the SAME /24
                   that has NEVER been forwarded to. It is what separates "the route
                   is gone" from "one programmed host entry is stale".
    203.0.113.9    CONTROL. No route for it exists in any VRF, in any leg.
    192.168.10.1   INSTRUMENT CONTROL. The DUT's own vlan10 address -- the SAME VRF
                   as the sender, so it must be answered in EVERY leg. A zero
                   elsewhere is then a measurement and not a dead instrument.
  A background ARP responder runs on both NICs, because this DUT does not learn a
  neighbour from a request it merely answers.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE CLAIMS UNDER TEST, QUOTED FROM THE PRODUCT DOCUMENTATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  VRF-lite Feature Overview and Configuration Guide, C613-22142-00 REV B.

  G1 "One or more Layer 3 interfaces (VLAN) are associated with each VRF instance
      forming an isolated VRF routing domain. A Layer 3 interface cannot belong to
      more than one VRF instance at any time."                              (p.6)
  G2 "VRF-Lite provides network isolation on a single device at Layer 3. ... This
      separation of the routing tables prevents communication to Layer 3 interfaces
      in other VRF domains on the same device."                             (p.7)
  G3 "The same or overlapping IP addresses can be present in different route table
      instances without conflicting. All routing table instances remain securely
      isolated from those existing in other routing tables."                (p.11)
  G4 "By default, no communication occurs between VRF instances, facilitating
      multiple secure routing domains within the same VRF aware device."    (p.11)
  G5 "However, inter-VRF communication between routing domains is possible by using
      either static inter-VRF routes and/or dynamic filtered route leakage via
      BGP..."                                                               (p.11)
      and p.17: "Static inter-VRF routing involves creating static routes in one VRF
      instance whose egress VLAN is in a different VRF. These static routes must
      specify both the egress VLAN and next hop IP address."
  G6 "As Layer 3 interfaces are moved to the created VRF instances, they are removed
      from the global VRF domain, so the global VRF domain manages a decreasing set
      of Layer 3 interfaces."                                               (p.7)
  G7 "IP configuration on the interface in the previous VRF is unset (removed)
      before moving the interface to a new VRF. ARP entries associated with the
      Layer 3 interface are cleared when the interface is moved from one VRF
      instance to another. In addition (static and dynamic) ARP entries are VRF
      aware, as the same IP address can be used in other VRF instances."    (p.7)
  G8 "If the Local Interface (LO) parameter is not specified, one is automatically
      created for the VRF instance."                                        (p.14)

