STEP 7 -- overlapping addresses in two VRFs (tests G3)                    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the headline claim of VRF-Lite -- the same IP address live on two
  interfaces at once, each in its own VRF, which is impossible in a single global
  table. A configuration that is ACCEPTED is not a feature that WORKS, so both
  instances are then measured independently, each with the opposite NIC as the
  capture.

  Applied 23:35:19 -- vlan48, in VRF blue, is given VRF red's vlan10 address:
awplus(config)# interface vlan48
awplus(config-if)# no ip address 192.168.48.1/24
awplus(config-if)# ip address 192.168.10.1/24
  (accepted with no message of any kind)

awplus# show ip interface brief
  (global)      vlan41 10.0.41.1/30 · vlan61 10.0.61.1/30
  [VRF: red]    lo1 unassigned · vlan10 192.168.10.1/24
  [VRF: blue]   lo2 unassigned · vlan48 192.168.10.1/24
awplus# show ip route
  [VRF: red]    C 192.168.10.0/24 is directly connected, vlan10
  [VRF: blue]   C 192.168.10.0/24 is directly connected, vlan48
                                           <-- the same /32 address and the same /24
                                               connected prefix exist twice, in two
                                               route tables

  Leg V7a-overlap-from-red (--iface eth3 --watch eth4, sender 192.168.10.5):
  cap eth3: 27 frames.   cap eth4: 0 frames
    x5 ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.10.1 tell 192.168.10.5
    x5 ARP-REPLY 192.168.10.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43          <-- red's vlan10 answers
    x5 ICMP t8 192.168.10.5 -> 192.168.10.1 ; x5 ICMP t0 192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.5
    x5 ICMP t8 192.168.10.5 -> 203.0.113.9 (CONTROL, 0 replies)
    -> 5/5 ARP, 5/5 ICMP, and NOTHING on eth4.

  Leg V7b-overlap-from-blue (--iface eth4 --watch eth3, sender 192.168.10.6 on the
  other segment -- chosen inside the target address's own subnet because this DUT
  answers ARP only for a sender in that subnet):
  cap eth4: 29 frames.   cap eth3: 0 frames
    x5 ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.10.1 tell 192.168.10.6
    x5 ARP-REPLY 192.168.10.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43          <-- blue's vlan48 answers
    x5 ICMP t8 192.168.10.6 -> 192.168.10.1 ; x5 ICMP t0 192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.6
    x5 ICMP t8 192.168.10.6 -> 203.0.113.9 (CONTROL, 0 replies)
    x1 ARP-REQ who-has 192.168.10.6 tell 192.168.10.1          <-- the DUT uses
        192.168.10.1 as its own identity on the BLUE side too
    -> 5/5 ARP, 5/5 ICMP, and NOTHING on eth3.

  Worth knowing when reading this: both instances answer with the SAME MAC, because
  all SVIs on this platform share one. So two hosts in two different VRFs see the
  identical IP and the identical MAC for their default gateway and cannot tell from
  the wire that they are talking to different routing domains. That is correct
  VRF-Lite behaviour on a one-MAC platform.

  Reverted at 23:36:xx -- `no ip address 192.168.10.1/24` / `ip address
  192.168.48.1/24` on vlan48, verified `[VRF: blue] vlan48 192.168.48.1/24`. The
  per-device .cfg deliverables were captured at this point, with the case's setup
  applied; swi_d.cfg's VRF lines read `ip vrf red 1` / `ip vrf blue 2`, running-config
  writing back the automatically assigned LO interface numbers of G8.

  => STEP 7 PASS: G3 confirmed on the wire. One address, configured on two
     interfaces at once without a single complaint from the device, is answered
     INDEPENDENTLY on two different ports in two different VRFs -- 10 ARP replies and
     10 echo replies in total, 5/5 each way -- while each leg's opposite-NIC capture
     is EMPTY, so the two instances never see each other and neither leaks into the
     other's segment. The unrouted control is 0 in both legs.

